nclusions | Springer Nature Link (https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-11858-5_9)\nciteturn15search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; \n\n# Conclusions\n\n  * Chapter\n  * First Online: 02 January 2026\n\n  * pp 165–170\n  * Cite this chapter\n\nSave chapter\n\nView saved research\n\nHow Glass Changed the World\n\n  * Seth C. Rasmussen  ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0003-3456-2864^{3} \n\nPart of the book series: Perspectives on the History of Chemistry ((PHC))\n\n  * 141 Accesses\n\n## Abstract\n\nUp until the rise of organic plastics in the twentieth century [1, 2], there was no other material that had such a dramatic impact on the world as did glass. The previous chapters have attempted to cover the origin and development of this important medium from its Mesopotamian beginnings in antiquity through the rise of Bohemian glass in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. While this coverage should not be considered comprehensive, efforts have been made to present the main lines of development of silica glass, from its various early soda-lime compositions to the later rise of potash-lime compositions following the collapse of the Roman Empire [3, 4].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImperial Chemical Industries: A History: Volume 1: The Forerunners, 1870-1926 - Reader, William Joseph: 9780192159373 - AbeBooks (https://www.abebooks.co.uk/9780192159373/Imperial-Chemical-Industries-History-Reader-0192159372/plp)\nciteturn15search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; \nThe growing interdependence of the explosives industry and the armaments industry, before the Great War, is described\n\nPublisher\n    Oxford University Press\nPublication date\n    1970\nLanguage\n    English\nISBN 10 \n    0192159372\nISBN 13 \n    9780192159373\nBinding\n    Hardcover\nEdition number\n    1\nNumber of pages\n    580\n\n### Buy Used\n\nCondition: Fair\n\nA readable copy of the book which...\n\nView this item\